  <section id="chto-eto">
    <h2>What it is</h2>
    <p>Nerves are covered with a sheath of <strong>Schwann cells</strong> — like insulation on a wire. A schwannoma (neurinoma) grows from these cells. It does not invade the nerve but pushes its fibres apart, so the nerve can often be preserved during removal.</p>
    <p>Most often a schwannoma arises on the <strong>vestibulocochlear (hearing) nerve</strong> — in the angle between the cerebellum and the brainstem. It is called a vestibular schwannoma. Less often — on the trigeminal and other cranial nerves, spinal nerve roots, and nerves of the arms and legs.</p>
    <p>Multiple schwannomas, especially on both hearing nerves, occur in a hereditary condition — neurofibromatosis type 2.</p>
    <Scheme />
  </section>

  <section id="simptomy">
    <h2>Symptoms</h2>
    <h3>Vestibular schwannoma</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>gradual hearing loss in one ear — the most common first symptom;</li>
      <li>noise or ringing in one ear;</li>
      <li>unsteadiness, dizziness;</li>
      <li>with a large tumour — numbness of half of the face, weakness of facial muscles, headache.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>If hearing in one ear has declined without an obvious reason, it is a reason for an MRI.</p>
    <h3>Schwannomas of spinal and limb nerves</h3>
    <p>Pain, numbness or an "electric shock" along the nerve, sometimes a lump under the skin that hurts when pressed.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="diagnostika">
    <h2>Diagnosis</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>MRI with contrast</strong> — the main test. For the hearing nerve, a brain MRI with thin slices through the internal auditory canals is done.</li>
      <li><strong>Audiogram</strong> — a hearing test, needed to choose the strategy.</li>
      <li>For limb schwannomas — <strong>nerve ultrasound</strong> and nerve conduction studies.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="lechenie">
    <h2>Treatment</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Observation</strong> — for a small tumour without significant symptoms: follow-up MRI on the doctor's schedule.</li>
      <li><strong>Radiosurgery</strong> — precise irradiation. Chosen for small and medium tumours; the goal is to stop growth.</li>
      <li><strong>Microsurgical removal</strong> — for large tumours pressing on the brainstem, with tumour growth or worsening symptoms. Besides removal, the main task is to preserve the facial nerve running nearby. For this, intraoperative neuromonitoring — continuous checking of nerve function — is used.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Schwannomas of the arm, leg and spinal nerves are usually removed if they cause pain or compress the nerve. The tumour is separated from the nerve fibres under the microscope, preserving the nerve.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="posle">
    <h2>After surgery</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>Follow-up MRI — on the schedule set by the surgeon.</li>
      <li>After removal of a vestibular schwannoma — follow-up with an ENT specialist; rehabilitation if facial nerve function is affected.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

